4.16.m:
And my father is Himādri, a portion of Kṛṣṇa. What could be difficult for me to obtain, O Lord?"
Hearing Pārvatī's words, Śaṅkara himself was well pleased;
Smiling, his body bristling with joy, he spoke sweetly:

perform worship to the goddess Caṇḍikā dwelling at the root of the banyan tree."
Hearing the word of Kṛṣṇa, the cowherds worshipped the deity;
having enjoyed foods during the day, they slept right there at night with joy.

4.17.m:
For them, death is life, and living is worse than death,
for those deprived of a good husband, their hearts struck down with grief.
For those plunged into other sorrows, [grief subsides] in time through drinking and eating;

And thus the beautiful woman always remained on the chest of the stone.
The entire story of Tulasī has been related to you at length;
nevertheless, it has been told again briefly in this context, O sage.
